WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.813
An architectural model records selected design
decisions, not every fact about the program.

00:00:05.813 --> 00:00:10.988
Components, connectors, interfaces,
configurations and rationale answer different

00:00:10.988 --> 00:00:16.545
stakeholder questions. The web-system examples
show how a cache, application, database,

00:00:16.545 --> 00:00:20.953
object store and external services can be placed
in a shared picture.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.609
The important question is what each element and
edge means and which decision the picture

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:26.386
helps evaluate.

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:31.530
Choose detail and formality according to the
benefit of creating and maintaining the model.

00:00:31.530 --> 00:00:36.504
Include constraints on interaction, concurrency
or behavior when those constraints matter.

00:00:36.504 --> 00:00:41.589
Static structure and runtime state are different
perspectives, although a running system can

00:00:41.589 --> 00:00:44.519
also change topology through scaling or
redeployment.

00:00:44.519 --> 00:00:49.912
Functional and quality concerns can both be
modeled; “prints medical records” leaves speed

00:00:49.912 --> 00:00:55.065
and confidentiality unresolved until the
description specifies relevant conditions and

00:00:55.065 --> 00:01:00.339
evidence. Is a more detailed model always more
useful? No. Detail incurs maintenance and

00:01:00.339 --> 00:01:04.713
cognitive costs; select what answers
consequential stakeholder questions.

00:01:04.713 --> 00:01:09.769
Performance modeling can reveal a costly design
before a full system exists. Execution

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:15.060
graphs estimate scenario work; queueing networks
estimate contention; other approaches can

00:01:15.060 --> 00:01:20.352
use simulation or learned relationships. Models
allow early comparison of alternatives and

00:01:20.352 --> 00:01:25.349
resource needs, but their answers remain
conditional on assumptions about operations,

00:01:25.349 --> 00:01:26.995
infrastructure and workload.

00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:31.389
A plausible model is an engineering aid rather
than a performance guarantee.

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:36.273
Execution models in this unit omit resource
contention. Their service costs therefore

00:01:36.273 --> 00:01:41.215
provide a baseline, not the response a busy
production system must achieve. Finding an

00:01:41.215 --> 00:01:45.237
unacceptable baseline is valuable because
queueing will not rescue it.

00:01:45.389 --> 00:01:50.901
Passing that check does not prove adequate
loaded performance. Combine early models with

00:01:50.901 --> 00:01:56.475
prototypes and subsequent measurements, revising
parameters as evidence becomes available

00:01:56.475 --> 00:02:02.237
instead of treating modeling and testing as
mutually exclusive stages. If an execution graph

00:02:02.237 --> 00:02:07.248
predicts 100 ms without contention, is 100 ms
guaranteed under concurrent users?

00:02:07.389 --> 00:02:12.087
No. Queueing, interference and model omissions
can increase response time.

00:02:12.087 --> 00:02:17.724
Execution graphs make a scenario’s control flow
explicit. Basic nodes represent processing

00:02:17.724 --> 00:02:23.236
steps; expanded nodes refer to another graph.
Repetition nodes specify loops, case nodes

00:02:23.236 --> 00:02:28.935
select alternatives, parallel-and-join nodes
wait for all branches and division nodes start

00:02:28.935 --> 00:02:31.816
work that need not finish before continuation.

00:02:31.816 --> 00:02:36.177
These distinctions determine whether work
contributes directly to the response being

00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:36.644
measured.

00:02:36.816 --> 00:02:42.000
Reduce simple structures while preserving the
chosen metric. Sequential times add, a fixed

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:47.183
loop multiplies its body cost by the repetition
count and a case uses the minimum, maximum

00:02:47.183 --> 00:02:50.523
or probability-weighted average according to the
question.

00:02:50.523 --> 00:02:55.149
Parallel elapsed time can depend on the longest
branch only under assumptions about

00:02:55.149 --> 00:02:59.776
available resources and synchronization. Keep
best path separate from best resource

00:02:59.776 --> 00:03:04.625
conditions: an average path without contention
is still not an average loaded response.

00:03:04.625 --> 00:03:12.692
Two exclusive branches take 10 and 90 ms with
probabilities .9 and .1. What is expected

00:03:12.692 --> 00:03:18.440
branch cost? 0.9×10+0.1×90=18 ms, plus any case-
node overhead.
